# HQL design status

HQL remains in language design. These cards are the current state of that
design and the source of truth for it. Where an implementation is narrower than
the design, the card says so.

| Card | Scope |
| --- | --- |
| [[hql/core]] | Small typed functional core and domain vocabulary |
| [[hql/collections]] | Set, Seq, Map and `cards : Set<Card>` |
| [[pipes]] | Ordinary functions, composition and data-parallel execution |
| [[hql/knowledge]] | Semantic universe, the card family and presentation |
| [[hql/extensions]] | The extension registry, `import`, and the unbuilt module loader |
| [[doc-type]] | `Doc`, the header, `format`, sections, links and embeds |
| [[card-type]] | `Card` and its two kinds, the vault, and the `cards` keyword |
| [[data-type]] | `Data`, the tree type of headers and structured payloads |
| [[type-system]] | `:`, generic parameters and progressive type refinement |
| [[fields]] | Optional `?` fields, partial schemas, and the two metadata layers |
| [[link-op]] | Doc references and the `[[..]] -> [[..]] {..}` link operator |
| [[graph-type]] | `Graph<N, E>`, `Edge<S, T>`, and the document and knowledge graphs |
| [[orderable]] | `Orderable`, the card's own key, and the `take` contract |
| [[option-type]] | `Option<T>`, absence, and the optional-by-type doc reference |
| [[hql/presentation]] | Terminal presenters and the host default |
| [[hql/error-reporting]] | Severities, the report queue, `strict` and `collect` |
| [[hql/semantic-search]] | `semantic`, `Hit`, `Ranking` and the read path of memory |
| [[hql/std-lib]] | Placeholder for the standard library; a bootstrap non-goal |

## Current direction

Prefer ordinary functions, bare bindings and fundamental pipe syntax. SQL
clauses and Rust-like trait machinery stay out of the design. A trait is a host
mechanism an extension may use internally and it never reaches HQL's surface;
what would be asked of it splits into ancestry (`:`), capability (an extension
registers a function) and a partial schema over `Data` — see [[type-system]].
Extensions supply external capabilities and may be written in Rust. That
registration is now implemented: collection steps are the core's, `graph` and
`present` are a prelude a vault may decline, and retrieval is imported where it
is used — see [[hql/extensions]]. Module resolution is not, so optional
namespace `init.hql` files remain design.

Generic types are written with angle brackets — `Set<Card>`, `fn first<T>(xs:
Seq<T>) : T?` — and square brackets are reserved for sequence values and
indexing. `cards` is a `Set<Card>` in the current vault or namespace, and a
prefix of it is reproducible because a card carries its own ordering key — see
[[orderable]]. `knowledge` comprises
concepts, relations, propositions and evidence. Knowledge retains conflicting
claims and provenance; a graph is only one projection. Programs return typed
values, persistence is explicit, and presentation belongs to a separate layer.

`Card` is the base representation type of the knowledge domain, with two kinds:
`ConceptCard : {Card, Concept}` occupies a node position, and
`RelationCard : Card` represents an edge without becoming a node. The kind is
declared by `metadata.knowledge.type` and must be checked, not trusted.

HQL has **no field visibility**: no `pub`, `protected` or `private`. Values are
data and declared fields are queryable data, so there is no privileged inside for
a modifier to protect, and a subtype neither hides nor re-exposes what it
inherits. What a type may declare instead is that a field is optional, `metadata?
: Data`, and that it constrains only the paths it knows about — a partial schema
over an otherwise open `Data` tree.

`doc.header` is required; `doc.header.metadata` is optional, because authoring
metadata is a choice. `card.metadata` is a **different field**, assembled when a
document becomes a card from the authored block, system metadata, extension
contributions, `.hmd/**` vault state and other derived information, under
precedence rules [[card-type]] owns and has not settled. The card family is
recorded in [[type-system]] and fields in [[fields]].

## Decisions still needed

- Function/declaration grammar, bound syntax, variance, inference and pipe
  argument placement — see [[type-system]].
- Collection equality, Card identity, mapping duplicates and ordering contracts.
- Document schemas and error carriers; an explicit `match` in place of the
  implicit lifting of absence — see [[option-type]].
- Semantic constructors, derivation rules and constraint/satisfaction types.
- The name and type boundary between graph projection and graph presentation —
  see [[hql/presentation]].
- Module resolution, namespace inheritance and `init.hql` initialization policy.
- Extension transport, and whether an extension may supply anything beyond
  steps — a type, a renderer or a document source.
- The reduce side of the pipeline vocabulary and the execution backend that
  runs a program — drafted in [HQL-0004](../../proposals/HQL-0004/README.md).

The implemented slice is now larger than the literal core: statements and
bindings, strings and data literals, document references, the link operator,
pipelines with lambdas, a vault of Markdown and HyperMarkDown documents, the card
family checked at load, semantic retrieval, graph traversal, an extension
registry with `import`, and a reporting queue. The signed 64-bit and double-precision representations, the absence of
Int-to-Float widening, and the addition-chain limit remain provisional
implementation choices rather than settled language requirements. What the
parser accepts is recorded in
[the grammar](../../models/behavior/expressions.md); what it deliberately does
not is in the [bootstrap requirements](../../models/requirements/bootstrap.md).

Which findings the implemented slice settled is tracked in
[consolidation](../../status/consolidation.md) and
[inconsistencies](../../status/inconsistencies.md); this card records the
outcome, not the history.

## Architecture sources

The sources these cards were drawn from:

- [[design-direction]] and the [example corpus](../../../examples/README.md).
- [Knowledge model](../../models/domain/knowledge.md) and
  [integration architecture](../../models/domain/architecture.md).
- [Program values](../../models/behavior/program-values.md) and the
  [bootstrap requirements](../../models/requirements/bootstrap.md).
- [Bind versus apply in generic types](../../models/behavior/bind-vs-apply-in-generic-types.md),
  which decides the bracket rule, and
  [optional fields and optional values](../../models/behavior/options-in-types-and-fields.md),
  which decides `field?` against `type?`.

Read those sources before changing language design. Keep unresolved alternatives
visible until a decision is made; illustrative syntax is not implementation evidence.
